Polymyositis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Symmetrical proximal weakness, Absence of rash, Anti-Jo-1/elevated CK

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-034
Reason for Visit: Progressive, severe inability to climb stairs or stand from a low chair, culminating today in an episode of choking on food, requiring urgent physical assessment to rule out acute bulbar failure or an acute neurological event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with worsening symmetrical proximal weakness who has now developed acute dysphagia and choking. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was strictly required to evaluate for imminent airway compromise, assess the severity of the primary myopathy, and determine if immediate emergency hospital transfer was necessary for airway protection, as delaying until standard hours carried a severe risk of fatal aspiration pneumonia.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My thighs and shoulders have been getting so weak I can't lift my arms, and tonight I choked violently on a piece of chicken and couldn't clear my throat."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 6-week insidious, progressive, and symmetrical proximal muscle weakness. The patient describes a complete inability to climb stairs without pulling up on the railing, profound difficulty rising from a low chair, and an inability to lift objects overhead. Today, the patient experienced a frightening episode of dysphagia, choking on solid food, indicating striated muscle involvement of the upper esophagus. The patient completely denies any cutaneous manifestations (no rashes on the face, knuckles, or chest). Reports a dry cough and progressive exertional dyspnea over the last two weeks, raising suspicion for interstitial lung disease. Also notes generalized fatigue and low-grade fevers.
Functional Status: Confined to a chair; afraid to eat or drink due to the recent choking episode.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of aspiration pneumonia and falls; lives alone, making the functional decline highly dangerous.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious, sitting rigidly, and breathing with a slightly increased rate. Voice has a slightly nasal quality.
Physical Exam: Neurological/Musculoskeletal: Profound symmetrical proximal muscle weakness is evident. Deltoid, biceps, and hip flexor power is markedly reduced to 2/5 bilaterally. Distal strength (grip strength, ankle dorsiflexion) is completely preserved at 5/5. Neck flexor weakness is profound; patient struggles to lift their head against resistance. The facial and extraocular muscles are completely spared. Dermatological: Complete absence of any rash; no Heliotrope rash, no Gottron's papules (differentiating from dermatomyositis). Respiratory: Fine basilar crackles heard bilaterally on chest auscultation. Oropharyngeal: Gag reflex is present but patient exhibits a delayed, effortful swallow with a tiny sip of water. Diagnostic tetrad heavily supported: symmetrical proximal weakness, complete absence of rash, and a clinical presentation that practically guarantees a markedly elevated serum creatine kinase (CK) and positive anti-Jo-1 antibodies on hospital workup.
Environmental Observations: A glass of water is untouched next to the patient; advised strictly against attempting to drink further.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol for mild muscle aches with no improvement in strength.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Polymyositis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute bulbar and proximal myopathic crisis)
Differentials:

Dermatomyositis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete, definitive absence of any cutaneous manifestations (no Gottron's papules, no heliotrope rash), though the muscular and bulbar involvement is identical.

Myasthenia Gravis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The weakness is constant and progressive rather than fatigable, and there is complete sparing of the extraocular muscles (no ptosis or diplopia), which are typically involved early in MG.

Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of upper motor neuron signs (no hyperreflexia, no spasticity, no Babinski) and the absence of distal muscle fasciculations/atrophy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Aspiration and mobility failure)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of severe, symmetrical proximal myopathy that has now critically involved the oropharyngeal musculature, evidenced by clinical dysphagia and choking. Combined with the basilar crackles suggesting interstitial lung disease, this is a severe presentation of Polymyositis (likely Anti-Synthetase syndrome). The emergence of bulbar weakness places the patient at immediate, life-threatening risk for aspiration. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely mandated to formally test swallow safety, assess respiratory effort, and trigger an immediate emergency hospital admission. Leaving the patient unassessed overnight posed an unacceptable mortality risk, perfectly validating the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient is clinically unsafe to remain in the community due to severe dysphagia and profound proximal weakness. Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance is required for urgent admission, CPK testing, autoantibody screening (anti-Jo-1), formal speech pathology swallow assessment, high-resolution CT chest, and initiation of high-dose intravenous corticosteroids.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict Nil By Mouth (NBM) protocol: Completely ceasing all oral intake (food, water, medications) immediately; explicitly linking this absolute restriction to the prevention of food physically entering the lungs (aspiration) while waiting for the ambulance. Postural maintenance: Remaining strictly upright at a 90-degree angle in the chair; achieving this position actively utilizes gravity to keep any oral secretions from pooling in the weakened airway.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; acute admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance transfer organized. If the patient begins to choke on their own saliva, becomes acutely short of breath, or loses consciousness while waiting, the patient (or a neighbor if called) must dial 000 immediately and update the priority.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe muscle weakness, especially the swallowing difficulty and shortness of breath, strongly indicates a serious inflammatory muscle disease that requires immediate hospital treatment.
Clinical Course: With aggressive intravenous steroid treatment, the inflammation in the muscles can be halted, but recovery of strength is slow, taking months, and requires prolonged rehabilitation and immunosuppressive medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to eat or drink anything, not even a tiny sip of water, as your swallowing muscles are failing and fluid will go directly into your lungs. Wait for the ambulance in a fully upright seated position.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is aggressively attacking your own muscles, including the ones you use to swallow, as well as inflaming your lungs. You need an immediate hospital admission for strong intravenous steroids to stop this attack and keep your airway safe.
Actionable Steps: Do not swallow any food or liquid. Stay sitting strictly upright. Go directly to the emergency department with the paramedics.

